1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:06,680
Let's read the first chapter of the Korean
Constitution, the General Provisions.

2
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:12,400
In nine articles, it sets out the basic
framework of the state before anything
else.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,210
First, a quick look at the whole chapter.

4
00:00:17,510 --> 00:00:23,240
It defines the form of the state and its
sovereignty, its nationals and its
territory,

5
00:00:23,540 --> 00:00:29,320
then turns to unification and peace,
international law and the status of
foreigners.

6
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:35,420
It closes with public officials, political
parties, and the State's efforts for
culture.

7
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:41,310
Paragraph one declares that Korea
is a democratic republic.

8
00:00:41,610 --> 00:00:47,940
Paragraph two says that sovereignty
resides in the people, and that all state
power comes from the people.

9
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:53,250
So the very first article names both the
form of the state and who holds
sovereignty.

10
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:59,810
Article 2 leaves the question of who
becomes a national to be determined by
law.

11
00:01:00,110 --> 00:01:05,890
It also gives the State a duty to protect
nationals living abroad, as provided by
law.

12
00:01:06,190 --> 00:01:11,630
Article 3 defines the territory as the
Korean Peninsula and its adjacent islands.

13
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:17,040
Article 4 states that Korea
seeks unification.

14
00:01:17,340 --> 00:01:23,550
The means is a policy of peaceful
unification, based on the free and
democratic basic order.

15
00:01:25,150 --> 00:01:31,670
Under paragraph one, Korea works to
maintain international peace and renounces
wars of aggression.

16
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:39,070
Paragraph two sets the mission of the
armed forces as security and defense, and
upholds their political neutrality.

17
00:01:40,670 --> 00:01:47,100
Paragraph one: treaties made under the
Constitution, and generally recognized
international law,

18
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,370
have the same effect as domestic law.

19
00:01:50,670 --> 00:01:57,290
Paragraph two: the status of foreigners is
guaranteed as provided by international
law and treaties.

20
00:01:58,890 --> 00:02:04,530
Public officials are servants of the
people as a whole, and they are
responsible to the people.

21
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:09,770
Their status and political neutrality
are guaranteed as provided by law.

22
00:02:11,370 --> 00:02:18,900
Article 8 covers parties. First, they may
be founded freely, and multiple parties
are guaranteed.

23
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:24,720
Second, a party's aims, organization
and activities must be democratic.

24
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:31,470
Third, the State protects parties as
provided by law, and may subsidize their
operating funds.

25
00:02:31,770 --> 00:02:37,120
Fourth, if a party's aims or activities
violate the basic democratic order,

26
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:41,760
the Government may petition the
Constitutional Court to dissolve it.

27
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:45,440
The party is then dissolved
by the Court's decision.

28
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:54,480
Under Article 9, the State shall strive to
carry on and develop traditional culture,
and to promote national culture.

29
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:59,580
Here, to promote means to spread a culture
widely and help it flourish.

30
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:05,110
That covers all nine articles
of the General Provisions.

31
00:03:05,410 --> 00:03:09,490
Next time, we'll look at the rights
and duties of citizens.
